  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a proposed solar-cell design in which a singlet-fission (SF) layer is placed on top of a silicon cell and coupled to quantum dots. High-energy photons are absorbed in the SF material, creating an excited state that splits into two lower-energy triplet excitations. Curved arrows show these triplets being transferred to nearby quantum dots embedded in or near the upper layer. From there, the energy appears to be delivered into the underlying silicon, shown by red downward arrows labeled as two transfer pathways.\n\nIn contrast, low-energy photons are shown passing through the SF layer without being absorbed and continuing directly into the silicon cell. This highlights the main idea of the structure: the top layer handles higher-energy light by converting one excitation into two useful energy carriers, while silicon still collects lower-energy light that the SF material misses.\n\nOverall, the image emphasizes energy management in a hybrid photovoltaic stack. The SF material and quantum dots act as an interface that could reduce losses from high-energy photons and potentially improve how efficiently the silicon cell uses the solar spectrum.",
